content:
**Read two integers and print the sum**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int a, b;
    cout << "Enter two integers: ";
    cin >> a >> b;
    cout << "Sum = " << a + b << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
Input 4 and 6 gives: Sum = 10

*Source: 3.pdf*

**Grade with if / else if / else**

```cpp
if (mark >= 80)
    cout << "A";
else if (mark >= 60)
    cout << "B";
else
    cout << "C";
```
mark = 65 prints B. Even or odd: if (n % 2 == 0) is true when n is even.

*Source: L4.pdf*

**Scenario**
This code is hard to read:

```cpp
if (age >= 18) {
    if (hasLicense) {
        if (!suspended) {
            cout << "Allowed";
        }
    }
}
```

**Questions**
1. Rewrite it as one compound condition.
2. Which version is easier to maintain and why?

**Answer:** if (age >= 18 && hasLicense && !suspended)

**Grade message with switch**

```cpp
char grade;
cin >> grade;
switch (grade) {
    case 'A': cout << "Excellent"; break;
    case 'B': cout << "Good";      break;
    default:  cout << "Invalid";
}
```
Input B prints Good. Without break, B would also run the default line.

*Source: lecturenotes.pdf*

**for and while**

```cpp
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
    cout << i << " ";        // 1 2 3 4 5

int n = 3;
while (n > 0) {
    cout << n << " ";        // 3 2 1
    n--;
}
```

*Source: loop.pdf*

**Scenario**
This nested loop prints a star triangle:

```cpp
for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++)
        cout << "*";
    cout << endl;
}
```

**Questions**
1. Trace the output.
2. How many stars are printed in total?
3. How many times does the inner body run?

**Answer:** rows of 1, 2, 3, 4 stars. Total 10. Inner body runs 10 times.

**Average of an array**

```cpp
const int SIZE = 5;
int tests[SIZE] = {79, 82, 91, 77, 84};
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; i++)
    sum += tests[i];
cout << sum / (double)SIZE;   // 413 / 5 = 82.6
```

*Source: array.pdf*

**Define and use a struct**

```cpp
struct Student {
    string name;
    int id;
    double mark;
};

Student s1 = {"Ali", 101, 85.5};
cout << s1.name << " " << s1.mark;   // Ali 85.5
```

*Source: lecturenotes2.pdf*

**Swap with reference parameters**

```cpp
void swapValues(int &a, int &b) {
    int t = a;
    a = b;
    b = t;
}

int main() {
    int x = 1, y = 2;
    swapValues(x, y);
    cout << x << y;      // 21
}
```

*Source: function2.pdf*

**Task**
Trace the output:

```cpp
void change(int &v)   { v += 3; }
void noChange(int v)  { v += 3; }
int main() {
    int a = 5;
    change(a);
    noChange(a);
    cout << a;
}
```

**Answer key:** 8. change works on the original (5 + 3). noChange works on a copy.

**Write then read a text file**

```cpp
#include <fstream>

ofstream out("class.txt");
out << "Ali 85\n";
out.close();

ifstream in("class.txt");
string name; int m;
while (in >> name >> m)
    cout << name << " " << m;    // Ali 85
in.close();
```

